  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM Wednesday...\n\n1) Cold air damming regime will stay in place over the next few \ndays, with cool temperatures and light rain. \n\nThe cold front has successfully made its way through the region, and \nnow looks to be located along the NC coast into South Carolina. This \nfront should stall to our south and at the same time a low pressure \nsystem is expected to strengthen off the NC coast. This has allowed \nfor a cold air damming regime to set up over the area. Through the \nday today, expect cooler temperatures, with highs looking to remain \nin the low 60s in the northwest to the low (perhaps mid) 70s in the \nsoutheast. This will be accompanied by overcast skies and periods of \nlight rain/drizzle. A shortwave trough should approach and move \nthrough the region tonight, which could allow for increased rainfall \nin the evening to overnight hours. \n\nThe low pressure system will continue to strengthen off the coast \nand move to the north, which should help the CAD erode Thursday \nevening into Friday. The erosion should be aided by drier and \nsomewhat cooler air moving into the region by Friday morning. Since \nthe CAD looks to remain through Thursday afternoon, additional light \nrain and drizzle will remain possible Thursday as well. \nAdditionally, gusty winds look likely as the low strengthens off the \ncoast on Thursday afternoon. After the CAD erodes, temperatures look \nto slowly moderate each day through mid-week.",
